# zlib1g -- CVE-2026-76844
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# The finding is titled as an information disclosure by path traversal in
# webpack-dev-middleware, a Node.js package, but Trivy attributes it to the
# Debian zlib compression library. The image has no webpack and no Node.js
# runtime, so the described flaw cannot be present, and zlib1g itself is only a
# compression library linked by other programs, not a web server. This looks
# like a vulnerability database mapping error rather than a flaw in zlib.
# The Trivy scan of agent-base in the 9.93.0 Release run lists CVE-2026-76844
# (HIGH) on zlib1g 1:1.2.13.dfsg-1 as affected with no fixed version and a title
# that names a Node.js package; re-check security-tracker.debian.org and the
# Trivy database for a corrected mapping each release and remove this entry when
# one ships.
# TRACED: docker run --network none --entrypoint sh on the built agent-base
# 9.93.0 image: dpkg -l zlib1g shows 1:1.2.13.dfsg-1 installed; find / -iname
# '*webpack*' finds nothing; neither node nor npm is installed in the image.
CVE-2026-76844

# libexpat1 -- CVE-2026-115506
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# A denial of service in libexpat through 2.9.0: the billion-laughs
# amplification check in accountingDiffTolerated() fires too late, so a
# moderately sized XML document with a large DTD and nested entities can burn
# tens of seconds of CPU per parse. It needs attacker supplied XML handed to an
# expat parser. In agent-base the only dependent is git and the only git program
# that links it is git-http-push, the WebDAV push helper, which datawatch never
# runs (it clones and pushes over ssh and smart HTTPS). The datawatch binary is
# pure Go (CGO_ENABLED=0) and uses Go's own XML parser, not libexpat. The
# residual is the Python-based images: agent-aider and agent-gemini carry
# python3.11 whose pyexpat module links libexpat1, so any script run in a worker
# session that parses attacker XML with Python's expat-based parsers would hit
# this. No datawatch code and no aider code found by the grep does that, but a
# worker can run arbitrary commands, so reachability in those images is not
# ruled out and is recorded as unknown. The impact is bounded CPU use in a
# disposable worker container, not code execution.
# The Trivy scan of agent-base in the 9.100.1 Release run (run 42190519222)
# lists CVE-2026-115506 (HIGH) on libexpat1 2.5.0-1+deb12u5 as affected with no
# fixed version; the Debian security tracker (read 2026-11-06) lists bookworm as
# vulnerable with no fixed bookworm version; re-check
# security-tracker.debian.org each release and remove this entry when a fix
# ships.
# TRACED: docker run --network none --entrypoint sh on the built agent-base
# 9.93.0 image: dpkg -l libexpat1 shows 2.5.0-1+deb12u5, identical to the
# scanned version; apt-cache rdepends --installed libexpat1 lists only git; ldd
# of every ELF in /usr/bin, /usr/lib/git-core and /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu
# shows that only git-http-push links libexpat. The same two commands on the
# older datawatch-agent-aider and datawatch-agent-gemini 8.73.2 images
# (libexpat1 2.5.0-1+deb12u4) list git and python3.11-minimal; a grep of the
# installed aider package for xml.etree, xml.dom, xml.sax, pyexpat and plistlib
# finds nothing. Debian tracker page read: bookworm and bookworm-security are
# vulnerable, fixed only in sid 2.10.0-1. A built parent-full image was not
# inspected.
CVE-2026-115506

# libsqlite3-0 -- CVE-2026-119374
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# A heap buffer overflow in SQLite's FTS5 fts5DoSecureDelete() (SQLite before
# 3.54.0), triggered by a crafted database file whose FTS5 index has corrupt
# footer key offsets, when rows are deleted from that FTS5 table with FTS5
# secure-delete enabled. In agent-base the only consumer is gpg, which uses
# SQLite solely for its optional TOFU trust database with fixed SQL statements;
# it never creates or deletes from an FTS5 table, and TOFU is off under the
# default trust model. The datawatch binary uses modernc.org/sqlite, a pure-Go
# implementation (CGO_ENABLED=0), never the Debian libsqlite3. The agent-aider
# image is different: python3's sqlite3 module links libsqlite3, and aider opens
# a diskcache SQLite file stored in the repository (.aider.tags.cache). A
# hostile repository could commit a crafted cache.db; diskcache itself issues no
# FTS5 statements, but SQLite runs triggers stored in an opened database file,
# so a planted trigger on diskcache's own tables that deletes from an FTS5 table
# is a plausible, unproven path to this bug. That path was not exercised here,
# so reachability is recorded as unknown, not no. The impact is a heap overflow
# in the aider process inside a disposable worker container; the mitigation is
# that aider workers only clone repositories the operator configured.
# The Trivy scan of agent-base in the 9.100.1 Release run (run 42190519222)
# lists CVE-2026-119374 (HIGH) on libsqlite3-0 3.40.1-2+deb12u2 as affected with
# no fixed version; the Debian security tracker (read 2026-11-06) lists bookworm
# as vulnerable with no fixed bookworm version; re-check
# security-tracker.debian.org each release and remove this entry when a fix
# ships.
# TRACED: docker run --network none --entrypoint sh on the built agent-base
# 9.93.0 image: dpkg -l libsqlite3-0 shows 3.40.1-2+deb12u2, identical to the
# scanned version; apt-cache rdepends --installed libsqlite3-0 lists only gpg;
# ldd of every ELF in /usr/bin, /usr/sbin, /usr/lib/git-core and /usr/local/bin
# shows that only /usr/bin/gpg links libsqlite3; grep -a of gpg for sqlite3_
# symbols shows only basic calls (open, prepare_v2, step, bind, exec, close) and
# no changeset or fts5 strings; libsqlite3.so.0 contains the fts5 module. On the
# older datawatch-agent-aider 8.73.2 image: rdepends lists libpython3.11-stdlib
# (the _sqlite3 module) and gpg, diskcache 5.6.3 is installed in the aider pipx
# venv, and aider/repomap.py sets TAGS_CACHE_DIR to .aider.tags.cache.v<N>, a
# SQLite cache inside the repository being worked on. Debian tracker page read:
# bookworm vulnerable, fixed only in sid 3.54.1-2. A built parent-full image was
# not inspected.
CVE-2026-119374

# libsqlite3-0 -- CVE-2026-119379
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# A heap buffer overflow in the sqlite3changeset_apply() C API of SQLite's
# session extension (SQLite before 3.54.0), triggered by a crafted changeset
# blob whose table name repeats with a larger column count. It is reached only
# by a program that calls the session API and feeds it an untrusted changeset; a
# database file or an SQL statement cannot invoke it. The library exports the
# function, but no program in agent-base calls it (gpg's SQLite calls are
# limited to open, prepare, bind, step, exec and close), Python's standard
# sqlite3 module has no binding for the session extension, and neither aider nor
# diskcache references changesets. The datawatch binary uses pure-Go
# modernc.org/sqlite, not libsqlite3. No caller exists, so the vulnerable
# function is unreachable.
# The Trivy scan of agent-base in the 9.100.1 Release run (run 42190519222)
# lists CVE-2026-119379 (HIGH) on libsqlite3-0 3.40.1-2+deb12u2 as affected with
# no fixed version; the Debian security tracker (read 2026-11-06) lists bookworm
# as vulnerable with no fixed bookworm version; re-check
# security-tracker.debian.org each release and remove this entry when a fix
# ships.
# TRACED: docker run --network none --entrypoint sh on the built agent-base
# 9.93.0 image: dpkg -l libsqlite3-0 shows 3.40.1-2+deb12u2, identical to the
# scanned version; apt-cache rdepends --installed libsqlite3-0 lists only gpg;
# ldd scan of all ELF files shows only /usr/bin/gpg links libsqlite3, and grep
# -a of gpg for sqlite3_ symbols and for changeset finds no session or changeset
# call; libsqlite3.so.0 exports sqlite3changeset_apply. On the older
# datawatch-agent-aider 8.73.2 image: grep -rl changeset over the installed
# aider and diskcache Python packages finds nothing. Debian tracker page read:
# bookworm vulnerable, fixed only in sid 3.54.1-2. A built parent-full image was
# not inspected.
CVE-2026-119379

# zlib1g -- CVE-2026-85091
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# A heap buffer overflow in gz_vacate() in zlib versions 1.3.1.2 through 1.3.2,
# triggered when a program using the gz* routines in non-blocking mode calls
# gzprintf() or gzvprintf() after a gzwrite() stall. Two things keep it out of
# reach. First, the scanned package is zlib 1.2.13, which predates the
# non-blocking gz* support (added in 1.3.1.2) that the Debian tracker notes name
# as the origin of the flaw, so the Debian vulnerable status looks like an
# unversioned default. Second, no program in the image calls gzprintf or
# gzvprintf, and the few gzwrite users (dpkg-deb, wget, sudo log tools) use
# ordinary blocking files, not non-blocking descriptors. The datawatch binary is
# pure Go (CGO_ENABLED=0) and uses the stdlib compress packages, never libz;
# Python's zlib module in the aider and gemini images uses deflate and inflate,
# not the gz* API. zlib1g cannot be removed because dpkg, curl, git and others
# depend on it. A built parent-full image was not inspected.
# The Trivy scan of agent-base in the 9.100.1 Release run (run 42190519222)
# lists CVE-2026-85091 (HIGH) on zlib1g 1:1.2.13.dfsg-1 as affected with no
# fixed version; the Debian security tracker (read 2026-11-06) lists bookworm as
# vulnerable with no fixed bookworm version; re-check
# security-tracker.debian.org each release and remove this entry when a fix
# ships.
# TRACED: docker run --network none --entrypoint sh on the built agent-base
# 9.93.0 image: dpkg -l zlib1g shows 1:1.2.13.dfsg-1, identical to the scanned
# version; apt-cache rdepends --installed zlib1g lists sudo, wget, util-linux,
# apt, rsync, openssh-client, curl, git, gpg, dpkg and others; a loop over every
# ELF in /usr/bin, /usr/sbin, /usr/lib/git-core, /usr/local/bin and
# /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu that links libz.so.1 with grep -a for the gz API
# names finds gzdopen and gzwrite in dpkg-deb and wget, gzdopen, gzflush and
# gzwrite in sudo_logsrvd, gzdopen in sudoreplay and sudo_sendlog, and no
# program that references gzprintf, gzvprintf, gzputs, gzputc, gzbuffer or
# gzsetparams; grep -c gz_vacate on libz.so.1 returns 0. Debian tracker page
# read: bookworm is listed vulnerable, fixed only in sid
# 1:1.3.dfsg+really1.3.2-4, and its notes say non-blocking support in the gz
# routines was introduced in zlib v1.3.1.2.
CVE-2026-85091

# libgssapi-krb5-2, libk5crypto3, libkrb5-3, libkrb5support0 -- CVE-2026-107364
# severity: high; images: agent-base
# CVE-2026-107364 is in MIT Kerberos server-side code that runs only inside
# kadmind or the KDC (kadm5 RPC, the GSS-RPC transport and AUTH_GSSAPI handlers,
# the KDB keytab reader or KDC request handling). The image contains the client
# libraries only and no KDC or kadmin daemon, so the vulnerable code never runs.
# The Trivy scan of agent-base built from main on 2026-11-11 lists
# CVE-2026-107364 (HIGH) on libgssapi-krb5-2, libk5crypto3, libkrb5-3 and
